Fine-grained graphite electrodes (POCO AF-5, 1 µm) produced lower relative tool wear than coarser graphite (POCO S-180, 10 µm) in EDM of Hastelloy C-22 in the experiments reported. {{cite:S3#c1}}  
Larger-grain graphite gave higher tool wear while improving process efficiency, so graphite grain refinement is associated with reduced electrode wear in these studies. {{cite:S1#c1}}

### Comparative tool wear
Experimental work on Hastelloy C-22 reports that fine-grained graphite (1 µm) shows lower relative tool wear than coarse-grained graphite (10 µm). {{cite:S3#c1}}  
The same line of experiments found larger-grain graphite provides higher process efficiency but with higher relative tool wear. {{cite:S1#c1}}  
No passage in the supplied library directly compares copper and composite electrodes against graphite for tool wear on Hastelloy; the available comparisons are within graphite variants only. {{cite:S1#c1}}

### Wear mechanisms
Graphite grains detaching from the electrode surface were reported to affect discharge stability and process efficiency, implying a mechanical/particle-detachment contribution to wear that depends on grain size. {{cite:S3#c1}}  
Larger-grain graphite was associated with more grain detachment and correspondingly higher relative wear, while fine-grained graphite reduced that effect. {{cite:S1#c1}}  
General reviews note that EDM faces high tool wear caused by continuous sparking and thermal effects, indicating thermal erosion and instability of discharges as overarching mechanisms. {{cite:S6#c1}}

### Process-parameter effects
Discharge current and pulse duration were identified as the main factors determining MRR and surface roughness for both fine and coarse graphite, and pulse interval dominated relative wear of the graphite electrode in those experiments. {{cite:S1#c1}}  
Another study on Hastelloy B2 reported that current and pulse duration greatly affect electrode wear patterns and geometric transformation of electrode ends. {{cite:S4#c1}}  
A factorial EDM study emphasized peak current and pulse-on time as highly influential on performance measures, indicating that lower currents and shorter pulses are generally linked to reduced wear in the reviewed experimental designs. {{cite:S2#c1}}

