Direct answer: Peak current (amperage) and pulse duration (pulse-on time) most strongly affect material removal rate (MRR). 

### Peak current (amperage)
Multiple studies identify peak current / amperage as the dominant factor controlling MRR, with ANOVA attributing 86.43% of MRR variance to peak current in one factorial EDM study. {{cite:S2#c1}}  
Another Taguchi/ANOVA study likewise found amperage to be the most influential factor for MRR (99.66% contribution in that analysis). {{cite:S8#c1}}

### Pulse duration (pulse-on time)
Pulse duration (pulse-on time) is repeatedly reported as a principal driver of MRR alongside current, with experimental results showing pulse duration (together with discharge current) as a main factor determining MRR for graphite electrodes. {{cite:S1#c1}}  
A full-factorial/Taguchi design also ranked pulse-on time among the top factors influencing both Ra and MRR in cryogenically treated and untreated superalloy samples. {{cite:S2#c1}}

### How the studies align and differ
The sources consistently agree that current and pulse-on time are the primary controls on MRR. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S2#c1}}{{cite:S8#c1}}  
They differ in quantified contributions from current: one ANOVA reports 86.43% for peak current on MRR while another reports 99.66% for amperage, reflecting differences in experiment designs, materials, and factor levels in each study. {{cite:S2#c1}}{{cite:S8#c1}}  

### What these sources do not cover
