Direct answer: Across the studies in your library, peak current (amperage) and pulse-on time are reported as the process parameters that most directly and strongly influence material removal rate (MRR) in EDM. {{cite:S2#c1}}{{cite:Akıncıoğlu 2022}} 

### Primary EDM Parameters
Multiple experimental studies identify peak current and pulse-on time (pulse duration) as the dominant factors controlling MRR. {{cite:S2#c1}}{{cite:Akıncıoğlu 2022}} Nas & Kara report peak current accounted for 86.43% of the variance in MRR in their ANOVA on EDM of a corrosion‑resistant superalloy. {{cite:S2#c1}} Other work highlights discharge current and pulse duration as the main determinants of MRR and surface roughness when machining Hastelloy with graphite electrodes. {{cite:S1#c1}} The Ramabalan study likewise reports that current and pulse duration greatly affect electrode wear and cut quality, implying strong effects on material removal processes. {{cite:S4#c1}} 

### Electrode Material Interaction
Graphite electrode properties (notably grain size) modify how parameters affect MRR: larger-grain graphite gave higher process efficiency (higher MRR) but higher relative tool wear, while fine-grained graphite increased process efficiency and reduced tool wear in another study. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}} Both Nowicki et al. studies identify discharge current and pulse duration as main drivers of MRR for both fine and coarse graphite, indicating the same parameters remain primary across graphite types even though absolute MRR and TWR change with electrode microstructure. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}} Other electrode materials (brass, titanium electrodes in other studies) are associated with parameter‑dependent changes in MRR and TWR, but those reports emphasize that amperage/pulse are the most influential parameters for MRR regardless of electrode type. {{cite:S7#c1}}{{cite:Akıncıoğlu 2022}} 

### Workpiece Specifics: Hastelloy
Studies specifically on Hastelloy indicate that the same EDM parameters (discharge current and pulse duration) govern MRR when machining Hastelloy C‑22, with electrode material (graphite grain size) altering process efficiency and tool wear at the same settings. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}} Nowicki et al. frame Hastelloy C‑22 as difficult to machine by conventional methods and show empirical models for how machining parameters affect MRR for that alloy, indicating parameter sensitivities were measured directly on Hastelloy. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}} Ramabalan’s work on Hastelloy B2 links current and pulse duration to electrode wear and cutting time, which are directly related to achievable MRR in that alloy. {{cite:S4#c1}} 

### Process-Parameter Trade-offs
The literature reports trade-offs between higher MRR (driven by higher current and longer pulse-on) and increased tool wear or degraded surface quality. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S6#c1}} Nowicki et al. show larger-grain graphite yields higher MRR but higher relative tool wear and higher surface roughness, while fine-grained graphite reduces tool wear and improves surface finish for similar parameter trends. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}} Shastri et al. summarize that EDM faces the interlinked challenges of high TWR, low MRR, and high surface roughness and that various hybrid techniques are used to navigate these trade-offs. {{cite:S6#c1}} Ramabalan notes that unstable sparks at higher energy settings can transform electrode geometry and adversely affect cut accuracy, implying that parameter increases that boost MRR can harm dimensional fidelity. {{cite:S4#c1}} 

### Measurement and Experimental Methods
The studies use designed experiments with ANOVA, Taguchi methods (including Taguchi–GRA and Taguchi–VIKOR comparisons), full factorial designs, response-surface approaches, and modelling (regression, empirical models, ANN/MOGA) to quantify parameter effects on MRR. {{cite:S2#c1}}{{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S7#c1}} ANOVA is used to attribute percentage contributions of factors (e.g., peak current explaining 86.43% of MRR variance in one study). {{cite:S2#c1}} Several studies produce empirical or mathematical models to predict machining outcomes from parameter settings for specific electrode–workpiece combinations. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S7#c1}} Surface characterization methods (Ra measurements, SEM, 3D topography) are used alongside MRR and TWR metrics to capture trade-offs. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S7#c1}} 

### Where the studies disagree
The sources consistently identify peak current and pulse-on time as primary drivers of MRR, but they differ on how electrode material microstructure (e.g., graphite grain size) shifts the balance between MRR and tool wear—some report larger grain raises MRR at the cost of wear, while others report fine grain both increases efficiency and reduces wear in certain setups. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}} 

### What these sources do not cover
No passage in your library provides direct quantitative comparisons of copper versus composite electrodes versus graphite for MRR on Hastelloy. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}} 

### For your thesis
Use these sources to argue that peak current and pulse-on time are the principal controllable determinants of MRR in EDM, supporting that claim with the ANOVA/Taguchi evidence in Nas & Kara and the graphite‑Hastelloy experiments by Nowicki et al. {{cite:S2#c1}}{{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}} Highlight the graphite grain‑size findings when discussing electrode material interactions, but note the absence of direct copper/composite vs graphite comparisons on Hastelloy and add sources that compare those electrode materials experimentally if you need that contrast. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}}

