Heat treatment of SLM-printed maraging steel transforms the as-built fine martensitic microstructure by promoting precipitate formation, relieving residual stresses, and—under intercritical conditions—reversing martensite to austenite, with corresponding changes in hardness and corrosion-related behavior. {{cite:S3#c1}}

Microstructure of the as-built state: SLM produces a very fine martensitic matrix with submicron grains and a nearly fully dense part structure. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S8#c1}}

Precipitation and age hardening: Aging causes formation of massive needle-shaped nanoprecipitates Ni3X (X = Ti, Al, Mo) inside the martensitic matrix, which are responsible for age hardening through Orowan and coherency-strain mechanisms. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}}

Residual stress and anisotropy: Heat treatments significantly relieve residual stresses introduced by SLM and can slightly reduce the layer-wise mechanical anisotropy of printed parts. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}}

Reversed austenite formation: Intercritical tempering near the α' + γ range (example: 720°C) precipitates reversed austenite preferentially along martensite lath boundaries, driven by local Ni enrichment from dissolution of intermetallics. {{cite:S4#c1}} {{cite:S4#c1}}

Effects on hardness and measured properties: Aging yields maximum nano-hardness in SLM maraging steel, and heat treatment broadly aligns nano-hardness of SLM and wrought material in heat-treated conditions. {{cite:S8#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}}

Implications for surface/thermochemical treatments: Prior heat treatments alter the response to subsequent processes such as plasma nitriding, where untreated material produced cracked compound layers but prior aging/solution treatments led to improved nitrided-layer morphology and better wear and corrosion resistance. {{cite:S2#c1}} {{cite:S2#c1}}

Mechanistic overview from reviews: AM intrinsically induces in situ tempering and precipitation phenomena in PH/maraging steels, producing hierarchical subgrains and fine precipitates that change strength, wear and corrosion behaviour compared to conventionally produced steels. {{cite:S6#c1}}

### What these sources do not cover
Quantitative kinetics of specific precipitate evolution during defined heat-treatment cycles is not detailed in these passages.

