Heat treatments applied to selective laser melted maraging steel modify the as-built fine martensitic microstructure mainly by promoting precipitation (age hardening), relieving residual stresses, and in some conditions inducing martensite-to-austenite reversion (reversed austenite). {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S4#c1}}

### Heat treatment effects
Heat treatments relieve residual stress and can reduce mechanical anisotropy produced by the layer-wise SLM build strategy. {{cite:S3#c1}} Heat-treated SLM maraging steel shows increased hardness through age hardening compared with the as-fabricated condition. {{cite:S3#c1}} Solution treatment or prior homogenization before subsequent thermochemical processing (nitriding) changes the response of the surface layer and can prevent defects in compound layers formed by subsequent treatments. {{cite:S2#c1}} Aging and tempering treatments also alter nano-hardness and corrosion potential compared to as-printed and wrought counterparts. {{cite:S8#c1}} 

### Austenite reversion
Intercritical or tempering treatments near the austenite formation range promote martensite-to-austenite reversion that nucleates principally at martensite lath boundaries. {{cite:S4#c1}} Local enrichment in Ni due to dissolution of intermetallics assists nucleation of reversed austenite during such heat treatments. {{cite:S4#c1}} Studies report that reversed austenite formed by intercritical tempering improves pitting resistance relative to as-printed, aged, and homogenized specimens that lack reversed austenite. {{cite:S4#c1}} 

### Precipitation and phases
Aging of SLM maraging steel produces dense distributions of needle-shaped nanoprecipitates identified as Ni3X (X = Ti, Al, Mo) within the martensitic matrix, and these precipitates are responsible for age hardening. {{cite:S3#c1}} The rapid solidification inherent to SLM produces very fine submicron grains and a martensitic matrix in the as-built condition, which together with in-situ tempering/precipitation phenomena gives a unique hierarchical microstructure relative to conventional routes. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S6#c1}} Prior solution treatment, aging and their combination affect the morphology and chemical composition of subsequent thermochemical layers such as nitrides. {{cite:S2#c1}} Precipitation is indicated as the main factor controlling both hardness evolution and corrosion behaviour after heat treatment. {{cite:S8#c1}} 

### Corrosion implications
Heat-treatment-induced precipitation and microstructural changes influence corrosion behaviour, with aging often reducing corrosion potential while increasing hardness. {{cite:S8#c1}} Reversed austenite produced by intercritical tempering was reported to raise pitting potential and lower corrosion current density and corrosion rate in 3.5 wt% NaCl compared with as-printed, aged, and homogenized samples. {{cite:S4#c1}} Combining prior heat treatments with plasma nitriding yields improved wear and corrosion resistance of AM maraging steel compared with nitriding without prior heat treatment, whereas nitriding without prior aging can lead to cracks in the compound layer. {{cite:S2#c1}} 

### Where the studies disagree
The sources consistently report that precipitation and residual-stress relief occur during heat treatments, but they vary in emphasis on reversed austenite: some studies highlight reversed austenite as beneficial for pitting resistance after intercritical tempering, while reviews note that in-situ tempering and precipitation phenomena during AM produce complex effects on properties without specifying uniform outcomes. {{cite:S4#c1}} {{cite:S6#c1}} 

### What these sources do not cover
None of the provided passages gives quantitative kinetics (time–temperature–transformation data) for precipitation or austenite reversion in SLM maraging steel. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S4#c1}}

### For your thesis
Use the microstructural findings that SLM produces a very fine martensitic matrix with in-situ precipitation and that post-build aging produces Ni3X needle precipitates to explain age-hardening behavior in the literature review. {{cite:S3#c1}} Cite the intercritical tempering study when discussing reversed austenite and its reported beneficial effect on pitting resistance, and cite the nitriding study when discussing interactions between prior heat treatments and surface thermochemical treatments. {{cite:S4#c1}} {{cite:S2#c1}} Add sources that provide quantitative TTT/CCT data and detailed electrochemical kinetics if you need mechanistic rates or activation energies, because those topics are not present in the current library. {{cite:S3#c1}}

