SLM maraging steel shows comparable or improved corrosion resistance relative to conventional material depending on heat treatment, surface finish and post-treatments, and reversed austenite and thermochemical nitriding can improve pitting and general corrosion behaviour when properly applied. {{cite:S8}} {{cite:S4}} {{cite:S2}}

### Effect of heat treatment and precipitates
Heat treatments that produce precipitation (ageing) strongly influence both hardness and corrosion behaviour, with precipitation identified as the main driver of changes in corrosion response. {{cite:S8}} Heat treatments designed from measured phase-transformation temperatures relieve residual stress and can modify corrosion resistance, as shown by systematic comparisons of as-fabricated and heat-treated SLM parts. {{cite:S3}}

### Reverted austenite and pitting resistance
Intercritical tempering that produces reversed austenite (precipitating mainly at martensite lath boundaries) increases pitting potential and reduces corrosion current density and corrosion rate in 3.5 wt% NaCl, indicating improved pitting resistance relative to as-printed, aged and homogenized samples. {{cite:S4}}

### Thermochemical nitriding
Plasma nitriding at 520 °C for 6 h can improve wear and corrosion resistance of SLM 18Ni-300 when applied after appropriate prior heat treatments, but nitriding without prior aging produces cracks in the compound layer that undermine the coating integrity. {{cite:S2}}

### Microstructure and AM-specific effects
The SLM process yields very fine martensitic microstructures with nanometer-scale precipitates and extremely high cooling rates, which contribute to age hardening and can alter corrosion performance compared with conventionally processed maraging steels. {{cite:S3}} {{cite:S6}}

### Surface finish and electropolishing
High as-built surface roughness (Ra ≈ 10 µm) is typical and electropolishing can reduce Ra by about 65%, which is relevant because surface roughness and surface state affect corrosion behaviour; after heat treatment, nano-hardness and corrosion behaviour of SLM and wrought maraging steel were similar in the studied cases. {{cite:S8}} {{cite:S8}}

### What these sources do not cover
Long-term corrosion kinetics in varied chloride concentrations and detailed mechanistic links between specific precipitate chemistries and localized corrosion initiation are not fully covered.

