Direct answer: Studies report that SLM-produced 18Ni-300 (maraging) steel can achieve near-full density and exhibits corrosion behaviour comparable to or in some cases improved relative to conventionally produced maraging steel depending on post-processing and heat treatment. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S8#c1}}

### Corrosion Performance
Overall reports indicate that corrosion resistance of SLM maraging steel depends strongly on processing and post-processing, with some studies showing similar corrosion behaviour to wrought material after heat treatment while others report improved pitting resistance after specific thermal treatments. {{cite:S8#c1}} {{cite:S4#c1}} Studies that combined thermochemical treatment (plasma nitriding) with prior heat treatment found improvements in wear and corrosion resistance versus nitriding without prior ageing, which produced defects in the compound layer and degraded performance. {{cite:S2#c1}} Results are presented for 18Ni-300 maraging steel produced by SLM or equivalent grade MS300 in composite form. {{cite:S2#c1}} {{cite:S7#c1}}

### As-built Microstructure Effects
SLM as-printed maraging steel shows a very fine martensitic microstructure with submicron grains and needle-shaped nanoprecipitates present due to rapid cooling, and the as-printed condition exhibits high nano-hardness and distinct surface roughness that influence corrosion. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S8#c1}} The as-printed microstructure is described as fine-grained martensite without strong texture or anisotropy across build sections, and this fine microstructure is implicated in mechanical and electrochemical performance. {{cite:S8#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}} High surface roughness (Ra ≈ 10 µm) in the as-printed condition is reported and is noted to affect corrosion behaviour until surface finishing (electropolishing) is applied. {{cite:S8#c1}}

### Heat Treatment Influence
Ageing and solution-type heat treatments modify precipitation, residual stress and hardness and thereby alter corrosion response, with ageing producing peak hardness and lowering corrosion potential in at least one study. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S8#c1}} Studies show that performing prior heat treatment before nitriding yields a nitride layer with fewer cracks and better corrosion resistance than nitriding without prior ageing. {{cite:S2#c1}} One study reports that heat treatments relieve residual stress and slightly reduce mechanical anisotropy while also affecting corrosion resistance compared with traditionally produced maraging steels. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S8#c1}}

### Austenite Reversion Role
Intercritical tempering that induces martensite-to-austenite reversion (reversed austenite) is reported to improve pitting resistance and reduce corrosion current in SLM maraging steel, with reversed austenite forming mainly at martensite lath boundaries and associated with local Ni enrichment. {{cite:S4#c1}} {{cite:S4#c1}} The 720°C-tempered specimen in that study exhibited a higher pitting potential and lower corrosion rate than as-printed, aged, and homogenized specimens without reversed austenite. {{cite:S4#c1}}

### Testing Methods and Comparisons
Electrochemical techniques reported include potentiodynamic polarization and open-circuit potential measurements in 3.5 wt% NaCl to assess pitting behaviour and corrosion current density. {{cite:S4#c1}} Immersion and comparative corrosion characterization after thermochemical treatments (nitriding after different heat treatments) were used to compare SLM and conventionally produced 18Ni-300 maraging steel, showing that comparable or improved corrosion resistance can be achieved when appropriate pre-treatments are applied. {{cite:S2#c1}} {{cite:S8#c1}} One study includes comprehensive corrosion resistance characterization alongside mechanical and tribological tests when comparing SLM and traditionally produced maraging steels. {{cite:S3#c1}}

### Where the studies disagree
Some reports indicate that ageing lowers the corrosion potential while other work finds that specific intercritical tempering (reversed austenite) improves pitting resistance, indicating that different heat-treatment routes can have opposite effects on specific corrosion metrics depending on microstructural outcomes. {{cite:S8#c1}} {{cite:S4#c1}}

### What these sources do not cover
None of the passages provide systematic quantified comparisons of long-term immersion corrosion rates across a wide range of chloride concentrations or environmental conditions beyond the cited NaCl test and nitriding conditions. {{cite:S4#c1}} {{cite:S2#c1}}

### For your thesis
Use the studies showing fine as-built martensitic microstructure, high surface roughness, and the role of precipitation and reversed austenite to argue that SLM microstructure and heat treatment route jointly control corrosion performance. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S8#c1}} Cite the nitriding study to support claims that pre-heat treatment strongly affects thermochemical corrosion resistance outcomes. {{cite:S2#c1}} Include the intercritical tempering/pitting data when discussing austenite reversion as a potential strategy to improve pitting resistance, but note that ageing can reduce corrosion potential in other contexts and so heat-treatment effects must be specified precisely. {{cite:S4#c1}} {{cite:S8#c1}}

