Households most often face upfront cost and affordability constraints, gaps in finance/payment options and subsidies, limited technical knowledge and awareness, and institutional or implementation barriers when considering rooftop solar adoption. {{cite:S3#c1}}

### Upfront cost and affordability
High purchase and maintenance costs reduce willingness to adopt rooftop PV, with cost identified as a negative predictor of adoption in survey-based studies. {{cite:S6#c1}}  
Affordability is reported as the main driver of the type of solar PV adopted and a constraint for income‑constrained households. {{cite:S7#c1}}  

### Subsidies, finance and payment mechanisms
Government subsidies act as a primary motivator by lowering initial investment risk and thus help overcome the high upfront cost barrier. {{cite:S3#c1}}  
Flexible payment mechanisms increase uptake by enabling income‑constrained households to afford more expensive systems. {{cite:S7#c1}}  

### Knowledge, technical and awareness barriers
Technical barriers and lack of knowledge or awareness are significant inhibitors of household investment decisions in rooftop PV according to survey and modelling studies. {{cite:S3#c1}}  
Survey work in a pilot smart‑grid project explicitly probed consumer experience and space availability as implementation concerns, indicating practical technical/installation issues at household level. {{cite:S8#c1}}  

### Institutional, policy and implementation barriers
Policy design, implementation challenges and broader institutional constraints are identified as obstacles to renewable deployment in India and relevant to rooftop PV diffusion. {{cite:S1#c1}}  
Reviews of the renewable sector name multiple obstacles that require policy and institutional responses to scale adoption. {{cite:S5#c1}}  

### Other factors: grid access and equity
Grid access is negatively associated with uptake of off‑grid solar kits and home systems in one national analysis, indicating substitution effects between grid service and rooftop/off‑grid adoption. {{cite:S7#c1}}  
Adoption patterns also raise equity concerns (demographic, spatial and temporal) which can act as barriers for some groups and places. {{cite:S4#c1}}  

### What these sources do not cover
Detailed, nationally representative causal estimates of each barrier’s magnitude across rural Indian states are not provided in these sources. {{cite:S3#c1}}

