Direct answer: Households mainly face financial barriers (high upfront cost and limited financing), informational and technical barriers (low awareness, perceived risks and technical concerns), and institutional or policy-related obstacles that together slow rooftop solar uptake. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S7#c1}}

### Primary Barriers Overview
Studies identify a mix of financial, informational, technical and equity-related barriers that deter household rooftop PV adoption. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S7#c1}}{{cite:S4#c1}} Financial constraints—especially the initial investment burden—are repeatedly emphasised as a primary inhibitor. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S7#c1}} Knowledge gaps, technical barriers and maintenance concerns also feature as significant inhibitors across household surveys and modelling studies. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S8#c1}} Spatial and demographic inequities (who benefits and where systems are deployed) are additional barriers noted in qualitative and mixed-methods work. {{cite:S4#c1}}

### Upfront Cost and Finance
Empirical household surveys and modelling find that upfront cost is a major negative determinant of adoption and that subsidies reduce perceived investment risk. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S6#c1}} Flexible payment mechanisms or financing options improve uptake among income-constrained rural households by addressing affordability. {{cite:S7#c1}} Studies that combine survey data and econometric/modelling approaches report that low prices, low maintenance costs, and subsidies strengthen financial motivators for investment. {{cite:S3#c1}} Analyses across contexts emphasise affordability as the main driver of the type of PV system adopted, especially in rural settings. {{cite:S7#c1}}

### Awareness and Trust
Low awareness and technical knowledge are identified as inhibitors that reduce investment propensity in household-level surveys and SEM/ANN analyses. {{cite:S3#c1}} Perceived benefits, perceived risks, and belief in self-efficacy significantly shape consumers’ willingness to adopt solar PV in household survey research. {{cite:S6#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}} User experience and local familiarity with rooftop PV (from pilot surveys) influence attitudes and likely uptake, implying trust-building through experience matters. {{cite:S8#c1}} Mixed-methods interviews and literature synthesis also highlight equity and justice concerns that can undermine trust in who benefits from solar deployment. {{cite:S4#c1}}

### Institutional and Policy Barriers
Policy design and implementation matter: studies reviewing national targets and policy instruments note challenges and gaps in achieving rooftop targets and identify policy-related obstacles. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S5#c1}} Subsidies and broader policy interventions are shown to be powerful motivators that can overcome initial investment risk when properly designed. {{cite:S3#c1}} Pilot-project evaluations indicate that government initiatives affect uptake, but surveys of early pilots report implementation and outreach challenges that can limit effectiveness. {{cite:S8#c1}}

### Household and Contextual Factors
Household characteristics and rural context mediate barriers: rural residence, household income, and dwelling type are significant determinants of uptake and of the kind of system adopted. {{cite:S7#c1}} Education attainment is positively associated with adoption in some analyses, while gender of household head and other sociodemographic variables show heterogeneous associations with adoption outcomes. {{cite:S7#c1}}{{cite:S4#c1}} Context-specific surveys (e.g., Kerala samples and Puducherry pilot data) show that findings vary by state and local conditions, underlining contextual heterogeneity. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S8#c1}}

### Where the studies disagree
There is disagreement on the influence of social influence: one nationally representative analysis finds influential people insignificant for adoption, while other behavioural studies emphasise social norms and perceived benefits as important predictors of willingness to adopt. {{cite:S7#c1}}{{cite:S6#c1}} Differences stem from variations in data, geographic context and methods (national survey vs. behavioural SEM/SEM+ANN analyses). {{cite:S7#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}}

### What these sources do not cover
These sources do not provide nationally representative quantitative estimates that decompose the relative share of each barrier across all rural Indian states. 

