### Randomized trial evidence (BlueStar pragmatic RCT)
A multicenter pragmatic RCT of the BlueStar app found no statistically significant difference in HbA1c between intervention and control at 3 months (mean difference −0.42, 95% CI −1.05 to 0.21; P = .19). {{cite:S6#c1}}  
An exploratory, within-intervention analysis from the same trial reported that each additional day of app use corresponded to a $0.016$-point decrease in 3‑month HbA1c (95% CI −0.03 to −0.003), indicating a small dose–response association between usage and HbA1c. {{cite:S6#c1}}  
App usage in that trial varied markedly by site, suggesting contextual factors strongly influence uptake and therefore potential clinical effect. {{cite:S6#c1}}

### Shorter pilots and behavior-change studies (not definitive for HbA1c)
A 12-week pilot of an adolescent type 1 diabetes app increased daily blood-glucose checks by 50%, but the authors stated demonstrating HbA1c improvement would require a properly powered, longer study. {{cite:S2#c1}}  
A systematic review of mobile apps for health-related behavior change found many studies reported behavior change and reasonable retention, but emphasized the need for large, high-quality RCTs to demonstrate clinical outcomes; all included studies had some risk of bias. {{cite:S4#c1}}

### Why effects may be inconsistent
Reviews and qualitative studies highlight wide heterogeneity in app quality, features (education, feedback, personalization), usability, and user awareness, which can impede adoption and sustained use—factors that the BlueStar trial found to affect usage and hence outcomes. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S5#c1}} {{cite:S8#c1}} {{cite:S7#c1}}

