Short answer: People stop using diabetes apps mainly because they find them insufficiently useful, usable, or integrated into their care, and because contextual factors (site, clinician involvement, connectivity) and individual barriers (lack of awareness, low health/technical literacy, perceived low disease severity) reduce sustained engagement. {{cite:S5#c1}}{{cite:S8#c1}}

### Disengagement Patterns
Studies of health and diabetes apps report variable retention and low sustained usage rather than consistent long-term engagement. {{cite:S4#c1}}{{cite:S6#c1}} Some trials show a substantial proportion of interventions retaining 60%–100% of participants at follow-up across heterogeneous app studies, but intervention-level usage metrics can still be low (for example, median days of logins as low as 9 at one site). {{cite:S4#c1}}{{cite:S6#c1}} Qualitative work highlights that users often drop off after an initial period when novelty or external incentives lapse, with participants naming specific barriers to continued use. {{cite:S5#c1}} Overall the sources agree that attrition is common and that usage trajectories differ by context and app design rather than following a single pattern. {{cite:S4#c1}}{{cite:S6#c1}}

### User-level Determinants
Individual factors linked to stopping app use include limited knowledge or awareness of apps as health tools and low technological or health literacy. {{cite:S8#c1}}{{cite:S5#c1}} Perceptions of disease severity and motivation influence uptake and continued use, with people who perceive lower disease threat less likely to adopt or persist with apps. {{cite:S8#c1}} Socioeconomic and practical constraints such as rural connectivity and general practical limitations also predict non-use or dropout. {{cite:S8#c1}} Some quantitative analyses suggest a dose–response relation between days of app use and glycaemic improvement, implying that low individual engagement predicts poorer clinical benefit and therefore may be linked to discontinuation, but this is based on exploratory analyses rather than definitive causal evidence. {{cite:S6#c1}}

### App Features and Design
App features associated with sustained use include simple, time-efficient interfaces, real-time feedback, individualized content, and automated data entry or device connectivity. {{cite:S4#c1}}{{cite:S7#c1}} Conversely, many commercial diabetes apps emphasize reporting and reminders over personalized education or therapeutic support, which users report as limiting long-term usefulness. {{cite:S7#c1}} Usability, credibility, and perceived usefulness emerged as recurring themes from qualitative work as determinants of continued engagement. {{cite:S5#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}} Interventions that incorporated gamification or reward mechanisms increased short-term self-monitoring in one pilot adolescent study, suggesting design elements that reinforce routine behaviours may delay disengagement in some populations. {{cite:S2#c1}}

### Context and Implementation
Contextual factors strongly influence whether people stop using apps, including clinician recommendation or integration into care, site differences in implementation, and connectivity or technical constraints. {{cite:S8#c1}}{{cite:S6#c1}} Trials found usage varied markedly by clinical site, indicating implementation context alters engagement independent of the app itself. {{cite:S6#c1}} Professional involvement and endorsements increase satisfaction and uptake, whereas lack of discussion by clinicians corresponds with non-use. {{cite:S8#c1}} Broader concerns such as app quality, security, device connectivity standards, and the need for regulatory or certification frameworks are also raised as contextual influences on trust and sustained adoption. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S4#c1}}

### Where the studies disagree
Retention figures vary across studies and settings: some reviews report intervention-group retention commonly above 60% in diverse app studies while pragmatic trials and site-level analyses report low median active-usage days and substantial variation by site. {{cite:S4#c1}}{{cite:S6#c1}} These differences reflect heterogeneity in study designs, populations, metrics of “use,” and implementation contexts rather than a clear contradiction in the determinants identified. {{cite:S4#c1}}{{cite:S6#c1}}

